          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"ibsen-et-dukkehjem-003dfd3f3df5ffc6\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":0,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.97,\"analysis\":\"Rank directly avows that he has loved Nora deeply. The interrogative frame challenges whether making this confession was wrong; it does not substantially weaken the presented truth of his love. Nothing in the words undertakes a commitment, and neither the stage direction nor the emotional situation shows the words functioning as a reflex-like discharge independently of the avowal.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The clause rearticulates an immediately relevant confession and is syntactically embedded in a question, but its principal force remains a direct avowal.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Direct dramatic speech by Rank, expressed as an embedded clause within the rhetorical question asking whether his avowal was improper.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Rank defensively clarifies his romantic confession to Nora: roughly, whether it was wrong that he has loved her as deeply as anyone. “Var det stygt?” shifts attention to the propriety of revealing the love, while treating the love itself as real. In the wider scene, the disclosure disrupts Nora’s prior understanding of their friendship and complicates her intention to seek his help, but it does not constitute a promise or relational undertaking.\" ,\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"ev1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“At jeg har elsket Dem fullt så inderlig som noen annen?” presents Rank’s having loved Nora deeply as the content under discussion.\",\"supports\":\"Strong truth-conditional support: Rank avows a loving emotional state directed toward Nora.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"ev2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Rank follows with “Var det stygt?” (“Was that ugly/wrong?”).\",\"supports\":\"The question concerns the impropriety of the confession rather than casting serious doubt on the asserted love.\",\"confidence\":0.96},{\"evidence_id\":\"ev3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The only accompanying stage direction is “reiser seg” (“rises”).\",\"supports\":\"The passage supplies no positive evidence of reflex-like or uncontrolled verbal production; physical movement alone does not establish E.\",\"confidence\":0.94},{\"evidence_id\":\"ev4\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is marked affirmative and perfect, with the explicit-love membership review retained at 0.99 confidence.\",\"supports\":\"Confirms that the highlighted construction is an affirmative first-person love predication rather than a negated or hypothetical example.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"ev5\",\"source\":\"background_knowledge\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"In the wider scene of Ibsen’s Et dukkehjem, Dr. Rank reveals romantic love for Nora, complicating her plan to ask him for assistance.\",\"supports\":\"Supports reading the line as a serious romantic avowal with consequential context, not as a promise, rehearsal, or semantically empty token.\",\"confidence\":0.94}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":true,\"familiarity\":\"extensive\",\"confidence\":0.94,\"contribution\":\"Identified the dramatic situation as Rank’s romantic confession to Nora and clarified why its disclosure matters, while the core T/P/E judgement remains strongly supported by the local wording.\"},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the avowal of enduring or prior love. P is unnecessary because no commitment is undertaken, and E is unnecessary because emotional intensity and rising do not establish expressive/reflexive core force. No residual aspect of the core utterance requires O.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":null}"
